WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.760
The ending of the original Blade Runner has
been hotly debated by fans for years.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:07.810
A combination of vague hints from director
Ridley Scott and all kinds of studio meddling

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:12.030
resulted in viewers being split on just what
we were supposed to pull from the end of the

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:13.740
1982 sci-fi film.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:17.710
And now that Denis Villeneuve's Blade Runner
2049 has arrived, there's plenty more dystopian

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:21.000
ambiguity for viewers to pick apart for years
to come.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.160
Here are our best guesses and interpretations
as to what exactly — probably — the ending

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.779
of the film really meant, and where the franchise
might go from here…or if it'll go anywhere

00:00:30.779 --> 00:00:31.779
at all.

00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:35.300
And it should go without saying: major spoilers
ahead.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:36.620
Decoy Boy

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:41.590
There's a lot in Blade Runner 2049 that simply
goes unsaid, meaning that untangling and unpacking

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:45.800
some of the plot's details and revelations
is going to be a little fuzzy — just like

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:46.800
the original.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.130
But one thing seems pretty clear by the time
the credits roll: Ryan Gosling's character,

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:54.870
K, is the decoy Replicant made of Deckard
and Rachael's daughter, Ana.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:59.120
We can glean from the clues K follows throughout
the story that there is a decoy, and before

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:03.329
the big revelation near the end we're meant
to think that the decoy was a girl, and was

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:04.329
dead.

00:01:04.329 --> 00:01:08.540
Sure, the leader of the Replicant rebellion,
Freysa, never points her finger at K and says,

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:12.010
"you, guy with the weird face — you're definitely
the Replicant decoy."

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:17.040
Still, we can piece together just enough information
to know who K is, and what role he was always

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.310
meant to play.

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:22.640
K was implanted with Ana's memory of the wooden
horse and the orphanage, so if anyone followed

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.869
the clues to find the missing baby, they'd
end up finding K — the boy that was listed

00:01:26.869 --> 00:01:28.270
as alive.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:29.740
Genuine Joi

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:33.830
Blade Runner's whole schtick has always been
asking about what it means to be human.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:34.930
What defines life?

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:39.670
Is a person a person only if he or she comes
from standard babymaking procedures?

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:41.860
Are we more than the sum of our biology?

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:45.930
But in all the excitement of meeting K, unearthing
Deckard in Vegas, and lots of punching in

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:50.159
the rain, one character's journey into humanity
should definitely not be overlooked.

00:01:50.159 --> 00:01:55.250
Joi, played by Ana de Armas, is K's holographic
girlfriend, an artificial intelligence he

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:57.110
bought from the big robot store.

00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:01.869
As the story progresses, we see Joi transform
into a true companion for K, who even says

00:02:01.869 --> 00:02:03.820
she loves him right before her death.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:04.979
"We have to go."

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:06.140
"I'm coming with you."

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:10.521
Later on, K sees an advertisement for Joi,
and he starts to doubt whether or not she

00:02:10.521 --> 00:02:13.160
— and her feelings — were ever real at
all.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:16.580
And those are the very same kinds of questions
he's asking about himself.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:21.320
Both movies ask us to take K and the other
Replicants' quest for humanity and personhood

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:25.490
as something that's plausibly attainable — despite
the fact that they're made in a factory and

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:27.740
implanted with memories and programmed.

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:31.550
And if we do, then we have to also believe
that Joi is capable of the same growth as

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:36.130
an actual person, regardless of whether or
not she's got an actual body.

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:37.390
What's in a name?

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:41.440
It's impossible to look at the names of two
of Blade Runner 2049's most prominent female

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:46.170
characters — Joi and Luv — and not see
them as highly symbolic.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:50.740
Joi was something that K longed for, and could
only find as a prepackaged product that he

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:51.740
could buy.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:55.220
Ultimately, Joi was something he could only
see and hear, but never actually touch.

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:58.230
And by the end, his Joi is crushed by Luv.

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:02.400
And Luv, as we see throughout the film, is
both beautiful and cruel.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:07.690
By the end, K literally grapples with Luv
and drowns her, all to save Deckard and reunite

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:08.690
him with his daughter.

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:13.210
For Deckard's love to live, he has to destroy
Luv himself.

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:14.680
The Deckard question

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:19.820
If you thought Blade Runner 2049 would settle
the Deckard debate once and for all…well,

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:20.820
think again.

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:24.710
In terms of the original Blade Runner, director
Ridley Scott says Deckard is a replicant,

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:26.870
while actor Harrison Ford says he isn't.

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:30.870
And even though the various non-theatrical
cuts of the film heavily imply that Deckard's

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:33.830
memories are implants, we're never told for
sure.

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:38.650
Well, don't find out in Blade Runner 2049
either — but there are plenty of insinuations

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:41.530
and implications that let viewers speculate.

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:42.980
For instance, Deckard tells K

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:46.120
"We were being hunted!"

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:49.430
That certainly implies that they were two
rogue skin-jobs on the run.

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:53.930
But the fact that he loved Rachael and then
went and fathered her robo-baby is also enough

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:58.500
to make anyone — replicant, human, or otherwise
— stay in hiding.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:03.400
Wallace, too, suggests that Deckard was programmed
to meet and fall in love with Rachael so they

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:04.950
could create a baby together.

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:08.730
But we never get confirmation that this was
what happened in the original movie, and we're

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:10.110
left to keep speculating.

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:13.700
But it's better that we don't know for sure
whether Deckard is a Replicant.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:17.410
After all, the main point of these films is
to ask the question of what it means to be

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:18.410
human, right?

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:21.510
It's not really necessary to be told about
Deckard's origins.

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:25.070
It's far more interesting and symbolically
effective to leave it ambiguous.

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:28.630
In fact, answering the question would kinda
ruin the movie, wouldn't it?

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:29.630
Another sequel?

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:31.820
They Repli-can't resist

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:35.290
If this latest sequel performs well — and
its excellent reviews seem to indicate it

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:39.450
will — there's no reason to think the studio
won't hop to work on making a third movie.

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:43.710
One of the best aspects of this film was the
way it continued to build the world we only

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.820
saw hints of in Ridley Scott's original.

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:50.000
And leaving Deckard and his daughter Ana alive
at the end of the film means Wallace could

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.010
still find and capture them for his nefarious
ends.

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:56.150
Plus, we've got a whole Replicant rebellion
waiting to kick off.

00:04:56.150 --> 00:04:59.810
Better reserve your ticket for Blade Runner
3: Revenge of the Replicants now, before they

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:00.810
sell out.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:01.810
Thanks for watching!

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:04.160
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:06.720
Plus check out all this other cool stuff we
